\title{Upsampling}
\description{
Upsamples a given multi-channel 1D (temporal), 2D (spatial) or 3D (volumetric) data.
The input data is assumed to be of the form minibatch x channels x optional depth x
optional height] x width. Hence, for spatial inputs, we expect a 4D Tensor and for
volumetric inputs, we expect a 5D Tensor.
}
\section{nn_module}{

Calls \code{\link[torch:nn_upsample]{torch::nn_upsample()}} when trained.
}

\section{Parameters}{

\itemize{
\item \code{size} :: \code{integer()}\cr
The spatial extents of the output, either a single number or one per spatial dimension.
Exactly one of \code{size} and \code{scale_factor} has to be set.
\item \code{scale_factor} :: \code{numeric()}\cr
The factor by which the spatial extents of the input are multiplied, either a single number or
one per spatial dimension. The resulting extents are rounded down.
Exactly one of \code{size} and \code{scale_factor} has to be set.
\item \code{mode} :: \code{character(1)}\cr
The interpolation method, one of \code{"nearest"}, \code{"linear"}, \code{"bilinear"}, \code{"bicubic"} or
\code{"trilinear"}. All but \code{"nearest"} are defined for one number of spatial dimensions only:
\code{"linear"} for one, \code{"bilinear"} and \code{"bicubic"} for two and \code{"trilinear"} for three.
Default is \code{"nearest"}.
\item \code{align_corners} :: \code{logical(1)}\cr
Whether the corner pixels of input and output are aligned, which only has an effect for the
interpolating modes. Default is \code{NULL}, which torch treats as \code{FALSE}.
}
}

